## 2. Repository layout

```
Cargo.toml                 workspace, version 3.0.0, dependency pins, dev/release profiles
cli/                       the `engine` binary, command routing, test runner, delegation
crates/diagnostics/        Span + Diagnostic rendering
crates/ast/                syntax tree
crates/lexer/              hand-written tokenizer, newline tracking for ASI
crates/parser/             recursive descent + precedence climbing
crates/semantic/           scopes, static checks -> typed HIR
crates/ir/                 IR model, verifier, printer
crates/ir-builder/         HIR -> IR lowering
crates/optimizer/          IR passes, re-verify after each pass
crates/runtime/            execution context, runtime errors, interp
crates/backend-cranelift/  IR -> Cranelift -> executable memory
crates/compat/             compatibility runtime: SWC transpile + Boa execute + CommonJS
crates/compiler/           frontend pipeline + end-to-end tests
examples/javascript/       differential examples with Node-generated .expected files
testCaseJs.js              full-JavaScript acceptance file
testCaseTs.ts              full-TypeScript/TSX acceptance file
docs/architecture/redesign-v3.md   the 3.0 proposal (preserved)
fix.md                     applied-fixes summary and acceptance evidence
engine.limitations.md      native subset + compatibility limits
.context                   this file
```

Native pipeline:

```
source -> lexer -> parser -> AST -> semantic/HIR -> ir-builder -> IR
      -> optimizer -> Cranelift -> machine code -> CPU
```

## 3. CLI surface

Bare `engine <file>` is equivalent to `engine run <file>`. `engine node ...` and
`engine bun ...` are aliases for the same routing, not emulations of those binaries.

```
engine [file] [args...]
engine run <file|script> [args...]
engine run -e <code> | -p <code>
engine test [path|filter...]
engine repl
engine check <file> | -c
engine inspect <file> [--no-opt]
engine build [args...]        delegated (Bun, npm fallback)
engine init [-y] [--force] [dir]
engine install bun [--force] [--version <v>] [--npm] [--dry-run]
engine install|add|remove|update|audit|dedupe|prune|outdated|link|unlink|pm|publish
engine x|bunx <package> [args...]   delegated
engine exec <cmd> [args...]         delegated
engine create <template> [args...]  delegated
engine upgrade                      Bun upgrade or install
engine --version | -v | -V
engine --revision
engine --test [args...]
```

Execution flags: `--interp`, `--no-opt`, `--threads <N>`, `--native`, `--compat`,
`--watch`/`--hot`, `--env-file <f>`, `--no-env-file`, `--input-type auto|module|commonjs`,
`--cwd <dir>`, `--preload|-r|--import <f>`, `--`, `-h`/`--help`, `--version`.

Test flags: `--bail`, `--concurrency <N>`, `--test-name-pattern <text>`, `--watch`/`--hot`,
`--preload`, `--env-file`, `--cwd`.

Behaviors worth preserving:

- `-p`/`--print` consumes the next expression and prints the completion value.
- Unknown flags before the entry file are usage errors; unknown flags after it are passed
  to the program.
- A missing file argument falls back to a `package.json` script of that name.
- Unsupported extensions are rejected (`.js .mjs .cjs .jsx .ts .mts .cts .tsx` are
  accepted).
- Stdin executes when it is not a TTY and no file is given; otherwise a REPL starts.
- `--cwd` is applied before `.env` loading and path resolution.
- Automatic `.env` loading walks the standard chain and never overrides existing variables.

## 4. Execution paths

`engine run <file>` behavior:

1. Read the source. Supported extensions: `.js`, `.mjs`, `.cjs`, `.jsx`, `.ts`, `.mts`,
   `.cts`, `.tsx`.
2. `compat::should_skip_native` skips the native attempt when it cannot succeed (`.ts`,
   `.tsx`, `.jsx`, module syntax, `import`, `require`, BigInt, private fields, async
   sources). This is only a performance hint.
3. Otherwise try `compiler::compile_optimized` (or `compile_to_ir` with `--no-opt`).
4. On success, run the Cranelift JIT. `--interp` uses the reference interpreter.
5. On a native frontend or backend error, fall back to `compat::run_with_options` unless
   `--native` was passed. `--compat` skips the native attempt entirely.
6. `--native` always attempts the native pipeline, even when the heuristic predicts
   rejection, so the user sees the real located diagnostic.
7. Native runtime failures do not fall back to compatibility (no double execution).

## 5. Compatibility runtime (`crates/compat`)

Purpose: execute complete ECMAScript/TypeScript/JSX programs while the native subset and
the 3.0 dynamic runtime are still incomplete. It is not part of the native pipeline and
is not the 3.0 implementation.

Dependencies (workspace pins):

- `boa_engine = "0.22.0"` — ECMAScript module/script execution.
- `swc = "80.0.1"` (default features off) — TypeScript/JSX erasure.
- `swc_common = "26.0.0"`, `swc_ecma_ast = "29.0.2"`,
  `swc_ecma_parser = "46.1.2"`.
- `swc_ecma_transforms_base = { version = "50.0.3", features = ["inline-helpers"] }`
  — makes transpiled output self-contained (no `@swc/helpers` imports).
- `serde_json = "1"` — SWC options.
- `icu_segmenter = "=2.3.0"` resolution pin in `crates/compat/Cargo.toml` — Boa pins
  `icu_normalizer ~2.3.0`, while `textwrap 0.16.4` (via `swc_error_reporters` ->
  `miette`) otherwise resolves `icu_segmenter` 2.4 and conflicts on
  `icu_collections 2.3` vs `2.4`.

Transpilation flow:

1. `.js`/`.mjs`/`.cjs`: source passed to Boa unchanged; SWC never runs.
2. `.jsx`: SWC ES parser with `jsx: true`.
3. `.ts`/`.mts`/`.cts`/`.tsx`: SWC TypeScript parser. A `.ts` file is first parsed with
   `tsx: false`; if parsing fails, it is retried with `tsx: true` because the acceptance
   file contains JSX despite the `.ts` extension.
4. SWC target `es2022`, `legacyDecorator: true`, React classic runtime, ES module output.
5. Erasure results are keyed by content and parser mode in a 512-entry in-process cache
   and an atomic on-disk cache. Cache root: `$ENGINE_CACHE_DIR`, else
   `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/engine/transpile`, else `$HOME/.cache/engine/transpile`. Set
   `ENGINE_NO_CACHE` to disable. Plain JavaScript bypasses the cache entirely.
6. Module detection: sources with top-level `import`/`export` (or `.mjs`/`.mts` extension)
   use Boa module semantics; everything else uses `Context::eval`. `--input-type`
   overrides the heuristic.
7. Modules are loaded with `load_link_evaluate`, which only links after every requested
   module loaded. Calling `link` directly panics inside Boa 0.22 when an import failed.
   The evaluation promise is inspected for rejection, then `Context::run_jobs()` drains
   microtasks and the timer queue.
8. `FileModuleLoader` resolves relative and absolute file imports, bare packages in
   `node_modules` (including subpaths, `exports` conditions/wildcards and scoped names),
   Node built-ins and TypeScript `paths` aliases from the importing file; it canonicalizes
   and caches modules, transpiles imported TypeScript/JSX with the same pipeline as the
   entry file, supports JSON modules with `type: "json"`, and returns a clean error for
   missing files or unresolvable specifiers. The entry module is registered from its
   canonical path so cycles and self-imports resolve to the same record.
9. Scripts are also parsed from a source path, so `import("./dep.js")` has a referrer and
   resolves relative to the entry file.
10. Unhandled promise rejections are reported through `HostHooks::promise_rejection_tracker`
    and fail the run (exit 1). A rejection handled by the time jobs drain (for example
    `.catch(...)`) does not fail.
11. Globals: `console` (all methods print), `process` (argv/env/cwd/exit/exitCode/stdout/
    stderr/nextTick/on), `setTimeout`/`setInterval`/`setImmediate` and their clear
    functions (a small macrotask queue drained after promise jobs), `queueMicrotask`,
    `performance.now`, `fetch`-free `document`/`React` stubs for JSX, and
    `structuredClone` via JSON.
12. `RunOptions.argv` becomes `process.argv`; `preloads` are evaluated before the entry.
13. Runtime errors are prefixed with the original filename. Boa appends `(native)` to
    errors without source information; `strip_native_marker` removes that internal marker
    because it does not refer to this engine's native pipeline.

Minimal CommonJS (`crates/compat/src/commonjs.rs`):

- `require` of relative/absolute `.js`, `.cjs`, `.json`, `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.jsx`, `.mjs`,
  `.mts`, `.cts`; directory resolution through `package.json#main` and `index.*`.
- Shared module cache; cycles see partial exports.
- `module.exports`, `exports`, `__filename`, `__dirname`, `require.resolve`.
- `bun:test` / `node:test` resolves to the harness API inside `engine test`.
- Bare specifiers and Node built-ins are rejected with an actionable error.

Critical implementation detail discovered while fixing `testCaseTs.ts`:

- SWC’s `Options::build_as_input` calls `Config::adjust` whenever the source file name is
  `FileName::Real`, and that forces `tsx = false` for any `.ts` path. Setting
  `options.filename = ""` is not enough because `process_js` uses `fm.name`.
- Therefore `transpile_once` creates the source file as `FileName::Custom(filename)`.
- SWC can error-recover and still return a program; check `handler.has_errors()` after
  parse and after transform and treat errors as failure so the TSX retry triggers.

Compatibility limits:

- Module resolution handles relative/absolute files, bare packages in `node_modules`
  (subpaths, `exports` conditions and wildcards, scoped packages), Common Node built-ins
  and TypeScript `compilerOptions.paths` aliases from the nearest `tsconfig.json`
  (JSONC, `baseUrl`). `paths` win over `node_modules` and do not apply inside packages.
- No import maps. No TypeScript type checking; annotations are erased.
- Namespace/named ESM imports from CommonJS expose statically found and live exports.
- `document`/`React` are stubs, sufficient for the acceptance file’s JSX section.
  Style imports load as empty modules; Next-specific rendering (server components,
  empty font-loader stubs) is not emulated, and CommonJS cannot require ESM-only
  packages. `URL`, `URLSearchParams`, `escape`, `unescape`, `fetch`, `Headers` and
  `Response` are globals; `module` and `tty` are available built-ins.
- Source maps are not propagated through the fallback.
- Printing uses Boa’s display/inspector; object formatting is not Node-identical.
- Module detection is a line-based heuristic. `--input-type` overrides it.

## 6. Performance work in this change

- `compat::should_skip_native` avoids expensive native frontend work for sources the
  native subset cannot accept, while `--native` still forces the real diagnostic.
- SWC erasure is cached in-process (512 entries) and on disk by content hash, with atomic
  writes; plain JS bypasses SWC completely.
- The watcher fingerprints only supported source files, `package.json`, and `.env*`, and
  skips `node_modules`, `target`, `dist`, `build`, `.git`, and other dependency/build
  directories on a 250 ms poll.
- The test runner discovers files once, runs them in parallel with `std::thread::scope`,
  captures output per file, and prints it in deterministic input order.
- `Cargo.toml` profiles: dev `opt-level = 1` with dependencies at `opt-level = 2`;
  release uses thin LTO and `codegen-units = 1`.

## 7. Commands

```bash
# Build
cargo build --offline -p cli
cargo build --offline --workspace

# Acceptance
./target/debug/engine run testCaseJs.js
./target/debug/engine run testCaseTs.ts
./target/debug/engine run testCaseJs.js --native   # expected exit 1
./target/debug/engine run testCaseTs.ts --native   # expected exit 1

# Command-surface probes
./target/debug/engine -e 'console.log(2 + 3)'
./target/debug/engine -p '1 + 2'
./target/debug/engine check file.js
./target/debug/engine repl
./target/debug/engine test
./target/debug/engine install bun --dry-run

# Full tests
cargo test --offline --workspace
cargo test --offline -p compat

# Differential examples
cargo run -p cli -- run examples/javascript/calculate.js
cargo run -p cli -- run --interp examples/javascript/fib.js
```

Build notes:

- First build of `compat` + `cli` takes several minutes (Boa + SWC).
- Incremental `compat`/`cli` builds are roughly 20–60 seconds (the profile change can
  force a longer one-time rebuild).
- `cargo --offline` works because all dependencies are cached.
- `/tmp` is a small tmpfs; use `/home/nishu/.cache` for scratch builds.

## 8. Invariants and constraints

- The native pipeline must keep rejecting unsupported semantics at compile time.
- The fallback must never change the meaning of a program the native pipeline accepts.
  Native acceptance is always tried first.
- `should_skip_native` is a performance hint, never a rejection.
- Keep the workspace at `3.0.0`.
- Keep `docs/architecture/redesign-v3.md` as the 3.0 implementation proposal; the
  compatibility runtime is explicitly not a substitute.
- Keep the differential tests passing; they compare native JIT/interpreter output and
  Node-generated expected output.
- Avoid adding more native syntax before the 3.0 dynamic value foundation lands.
- `fix.md` and the other docs must describe implemented code changes and evidence, not
  intentions.

## 9. Known limitations / next steps

1. Implement the 3.0 native dynamic runtime per `docs/architecture/redesign-v3.md`:
   unified `JsValue`, object/property model, distinct `null`/`undefined`, GC, UTF-16
   strings, TypeScript type checking, closures.
2. Replace the Boa/SWC fallback with the native runtime as those capabilities land; keep
   it only as a temporary compatibility layer until then.
3. Add bare-specifier/package resolution, import maps, and full Node built-ins iff the
   fallback is retained.
4. `fetch` is not provided; timers and promise jobs are (no full Node event loop).
5. Extend native lexer diagnostics to any other unsupported literal form that currently
   produces a generic error.
6. Consider creating one commit for this work; the tree is currently uncommitted on top
   of `5bc748d start v3`.

